steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1785329882
      nanos: 950715136
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "# Frontend Build Prompt (for Google AI Studio / Bolt)\n\nBuild a single-page web app frontend (React + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS) for a student job-search tool called **NextBuild**. This prompt is fully self-contained \342\200\224 follow it exactly, do not invent extra system-status text, jargon, or filler copy anywhere.\n\n## What the product does (for context only \342\200\224 build the frontend, not the backend)\n\nA student uploads their resume and links their GitHub profile. The app pulls a job posting they\'re targeting, checks how well their existing GitHub projects match that job, and generates a step-by-step roadmap of what to build next to close the gap \342\200\224 plus a ready-to-use application package (resume summary, \"why this role\" blurb).\n\n## Design Direction: Blueprint / Drafting Table\n\nThe visual metaphor is a technical blueprint / architect\'s drafting table \342\200\224 because the product turns a resume into a concrete \"build plan.\" This is a **color, typography, and diagram metaphor only.** Do NOT add fake system-status text, version numbers, \"NODE-01\" style labels, \"SYS_SCHEMATIC,\" \"AUDIT STATUS,\" or any sci-fi/engineering-jargon strings anywhere. Every piece of text on the page must be real, plain-language content that helps the student understand what to do \342\200\224 nothing decorative or cryptic.\n\n### Colors (use exactly these hex values)\n- `#10253F` \342\200\224 Blueprint Navy \342\200\224 primary background\n- `#3D6FB4` \342\200\224 Drafting Line Blue \342\200\224 diagram lines, borders, secondary UI elements\n- `#F2F0E6` \342\200\224 Chalk Paper \342\200\224 primary text color and light card surfaces\n- `#F2A93B` \342\200\224 Signal Amber \342\200\224 the ONE accent color, used only for primary buttons, active/highlighted states, and focus rings\n- `#7C93AC` \342\200\224 Slate Fog \342\200\224 secondary/muted text, placeholder text, inactive states\n- `#4FA87B` \342\200\224 Verified Green \342\200\224 used only for a confirmed \"strong fit\" status tag, nowhere else\n\n### Typography (use real Google Fonts, import them properly)\n- **Display/headings:** Space Grotesk, bold weight \342\200\224 used for page title, section headers only\n- **Body text:** Inter, regular/medium weight \342\200\224 used for all paragraph text, descriptions, form labels\n- **Data/technical values:** IBM Plex Mono \342\200\224 used ONLY for actual technical values: GitHub repo names, tech stack tags (e.g., \"React\", \"FastAPI\"), fit-score percentages, usernames. Never use this font for headings or general body copy.\n\nDo not render the whole page in one monospace font. The three fonts must look visually distinct from each other.\n\n### Signature element: Hero Blueprint Diagram\nBuild one hero diagram as an SVG showing 5 connected stages with a dashed connector line between each, in this exact order and with this exact copy \342\200\224 do not invent alternate labels:\n1. **Resume + GitHub** \342\200\224 small icon, label underneath: \"Add your resume and GitHub\"\n2. **Job Description** \342\200\224 label: \"We read the job posting\"\n3. **Fit Analysis** \342\200\224 label: \"See what matches, what\'s missing\" (this node should use Signal Amber to stand out as the \"you are here\" active step)\n4. **Roadmap** \342\200\224 label: \"Get a build plan for the gaps\"\n5. **Applied** \342\200\224 label: \"Ready to apply\"\n\nOn page load, animate this diagram drawing itself in (the connector lines drawing left to right, each node fading/scaling in after its line connects) over about 1.5\342\200\2232 seconds. Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` \342\200\224 skip the animation and show the diagram fully drawn immediately if that setting is on.\n\n## Page Structure (build these sections top to bottom)\n\n### 1. Navbar\n- Logo/wordmark: \"NextBuild\" in Space Grotesk, left-aligned\n- Right side: a \"Sign In\" button (Chalk Paper text, transparent background, Drafting Line Blue border) and a \"Get Started\" button (Signal Amber background, Blueprint Navy text, filled)\n\n### 2. Hero Section\n- Large Space Grotesk headline: \"See exactly what to build next to land your target job\"\n- Body copy below in Inter, Slate Fog color: \"Connect your resume and GitHub. We\'ll show you how your projects stack up against the job you want, and exactly what to build to close the gap.\"\n- The Hero Blueprint Diagram (described above) rendered below or beside this text\n- One primary CTA button: \"Get Started\" (Signal Amber, Blueprint Navy text)\n\n### 3. Resume Upload Section\n- Section label (small, Slate Fog, uppercase, letter-spaced): \"Step 1 \342\200\224 Your Resume\"\n- A custom-styled drag-and-drop file zone (NOT a default browser file input): dashed Drafting Line Blue border, Chalk Paper background, centered icon (upload icon) and text \"Drop your PDF or DOCX resume here, or click to browse\"\n- Below it, a secondary text-link-style button: \"Use a sample resume instead\" (Slate Fog text, underlined on hover)\n- After a file is selected: show the filename with a checkmark icon and a small \"Remove\" link\n\n### 4. GitHub Connect Section\n- Section label: \"Step 2 \342\200\224 Your GitHub\"\n- A styled text input (Chalk Paper background, Drafting Line Blue border, Blueprint Navy text) with placeholder \"GitHub username or profile URL\"\n- A \"Connect\" button next to it (Drafting Line Blue background, Chalk Paper text)\n- After connecting: show a small grid of repo cards (2-3 columns), each card showing: repo name in IBM Plex Mono, a short one-line description in Inter, and 2-3 tech stack tags styled as small pill badges in IBM Plex Mono on a Slate Fog background\n\n### 5. Job Description Section\n- Section label: \"Step 3 \342\200\224 The Job You Want\"\n- A styled text input for a job posting URL, placeholder: \"Paste a job posting link (LinkedIn, Naukri, Indeed)\"\n- A \"Fetch Job\" button (Drafting Line Blue background)\n- After fetching: show the job title (Space Grotesk, bold), company name (Inter, Slate Fog), and a short excerpt of the description in Inter\n\n### 6. Fit Analysis Results Section\n- Section label: \"Your Fit Analysis\"\n- A large overall verdict badge at the top: text like \"Strong Match\" (Verified Green background) or \"Partial Match\" (Signal Amber background) or \"Needs Work\" (a muted red, `#C4634F`, used only here)\n- Below it, a list of the student\'s existing projects as cards, each showing: project name (IBM Plex Mono), a fit verdict tag, and 1-2 lines of plain-language reasoning in Inter (e.g., \"Your e-commerce API project shows strong backend skills, but this role also wants ML experience which isn\'t reflected in your repos yet.\")\n- A small caption at the bottom of this section in Slate Fog, smaller text: \"This is AI-generated guidance to help you decide what to build next \342\200\224 not a guarantee of how a recruiter will see your profile.\"\n\n### 7. Roadmap Section\n- Section label: \"Your Build Plan\"\n- 2-3 recommended project cards, laid out as a horizontal drafted path (connected by a dashed line, similar styling to the hero diagram) on desktop, and a simple vertical stacked list on mobile\n- Each card shows: project title (Space Grotesk, medium), a one-sentence problem statement (Inter), tech stack tags (IBM Plex Mono pills), and an estimated build time (e.g., \"~1 week\", Slate Fog text)\n- Clicking a card expands it to show the full milestone-by-milestone roadmap (a simple numbered list is fine here \342\200\224 numbering is appropriate since these are genuinely sequential steps)\n\n### 8. Application Package Section\n- Section label: \"Ready to Apply\"\n- A card showing a tailored resume-highlight summary and a \"why this role\" blurb, each with a small \"Copy\" button (icon + text) next to it\n- A simple status selector below: three tappable pill buttons \342\200\224 \"Saved\", \"Applied\", \"Interviewing\" \342\200\224 the selected one filled with Signal Amber, others outlined\n\n## Interaction & Accessibility Requirements\n- All interactive elements (buttons, inputs, cards, the roadmap items) must have a visible focus state: a dashed Signal Amber outline, not the browser default\n- Fully responsive down to mobile width (375px): the hero diagram and roadmap path should both collapse to a vertical stacked layout on narrow screens\n- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`: disable the hero draw-in animation and any hover-transition effects for users with that OS setting enabled\n- Use real semantic HTML (proper `<button>`, `<input>`, heading levels) \342\200\224 not divs styled to look like interactive elements\n\n## Explicit Don\'ts\n- Do not add any fake status text, version numbers, \"system\" labels, or invented technical jargon anywhere on the page\n- Do not use a dark-mode-with-emerald/cyan-glassmorphism look \342\200\224 that is explicitly NOT the direction\n- Do not use only one font across the whole page \342\200\224 the three type roles must be visually distinguishable\n- Do not leave any default unstyled browser form elements (file inputs, buttons) \342\200\224 every interactive element must be custom-styled to match the palette above"
    items {
      text: "# Frontend Build Prompt (for Google AI Studio / Bolt)\n\nBuild a single-page web app frontend (React + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS) for a student job-search tool called **NextBuild**. This prompt is fully self-contained \342\200\224 follow it exactly, do not invent extra system-status text, jargon, or filler copy anywhere.\n\n## What the product does (for context only \342\200\224 build the frontend, not the backend)\n\nA student uploads their resume and links their GitHub profile. The app pulls a job posting they\'re targeting, checks how well their existing GitHub projects match that job, and generates a step-by-step roadmap of what to build next to close the gap \342\200\224 plus a ready-to-use application package (resume summary, \"why this role\" blurb).\n\n## Design Direction: Blueprint / Drafting Table\n\nThe visual metaphor is a technical blueprint / architect\'s drafting table \342\200\224 because the product turns a resume into a concrete \"build plan.\" This is a **color, typography, and diagram metaphor only.** Do NOT add fake system-status text, version numbers, \"NODE-01\" style labels, \"SYS_SCHEMATIC,\" \"AUDIT STATUS,\" or any sci-fi/engineering-jargon strings anywhere. Every piece of text on the page must be real, plain-language content that helps the student understand what to do \342\200\224 nothing decorative or cryptic.\n\n### Colors (use exactly these hex values)\n- `#10253F` \342\200\224 Blueprint Navy \342\200\224 primary background\n- `#3D6FB4` \342\200\224 Drafting Line Blue \342\200\224 diagram lines, borders, secondary UI elements\n- `#F2F0E6` \342\200\224 Chalk Paper \342\200\224 primary text color and light card surfaces\n- `#F2A93B` \342\200\224 Signal Amber \342\200\224 the ONE accent color, used only for primary buttons, active/highlighted states, and focus rings\n- `#7C93AC` \342\200\224 Slate Fog \342\200\224 secondary/muted text, placeholder text, inactive states\n- `#4FA87B` \342\200\224 Verified Green \342\200\224 used only for a confirmed \"strong fit\" status tag, nowhere else\n\n### Typography (use real Google Fonts, import them properly)\n- **Display/headings:** Space Grotesk, bold weight \342\200\224 used for page title, section headers only\n- **Body text:** Inter, regular/medium weight \342\200\224 used for all paragraph text, descriptions, form labels\n- **Data/technical values:** IBM Plex Mono \342\200\224 used ONLY for actual technical values: GitHub repo names, tech stack tags (e.g., \"React\", \"FastAPI\"), fit-score percentages, usernames. Never use this font for headings or general body copy.\n\nDo not render the whole page in one monospace font. The three fonts must look visually distinct from each other.\n\n### Signature element: Hero Blueprint Diagram\nBuild one hero diagram as an SVG showing 5 connected stages with a dashed connector line between each, in this exact order and with this exact copy \342\200\224 do not invent alternate labels:\n1. **Resume + GitHub** \342\200\224 small icon, label underneath: \"Add your resume and GitHub\"\n2. **Job Description** \342\200\224 label: \"We read the job posting\"\n3. **Fit Analysis** \342\200\224 label: \"See what matches, what\'s missing\" (this node should use Signal Amber to stand out as the \"you are here\" active step)\n4. **Roadmap** \342\200\224 label: \"Get a build plan for the gaps\"\n5. **Applied** \342\200\224 label: \"Ready to apply\"\n\nOn page load, animate this diagram drawing itself in (the connector lines drawing left to right, each node fading/scaling in after its line connects) over about 1.5\342\200\2232 seconds. Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` \342\200\224 skip the animation and show the diagram fully drawn immediately if that setting is on.\n\n## Page Structure (build these sections top to bottom)\n\n### 1. Navbar\n- Logo/wordmark: \"NextBuild\" in Space Grotesk, left-aligned\n- Right side: a \"Sign In\" button (Chalk Paper text, transparent background, Drafting Line Blue border) and a \"Get Started\" button (Signal Amber background, Blueprint Navy text, filled)\n\n### 2. Hero Section\n- Large Space Grotesk headline: \"See exactly what to build next to land your target job\"\n- Body copy below in Inter, Slate Fog color: \"Connect your resume and GitHub. We\'ll show you how your projects stack up against the job you want, and exactly what to build to close the gap.\"\n- The Hero Blueprint Diagram (described above) rendered below or beside this text\n- One primary CTA button: \"Get Started\" (Signal Amber, Blueprint Navy text)\n\n### 3. Resume Upload Section\n- Section label (small, Slate Fog, uppercase, letter-spaced): \"Step 1 \342\200\224 Your Resume\"\n- A custom-styled drag-and-drop file zone (NOT a default browser file input): dashed Drafting Line Blue border, Chalk Paper background, centered icon (upload icon) and text \"Drop your PDF or DOCX resume here, or click to browse\"\n- Below it, a secondary text-link-style button: \"Use a sample resume instead\" (Slate Fog text, underlined on hover)\n- After a file is selected: show the filename with a checkmark icon and a small \"Remove\" link\n\n### 4. GitHub Connect Section\n- Section label: \"Step 2 \342\200\224 Your GitHub\"\n- A styled text input (Chalk Paper background, Drafting Line Blue border, Blueprint Navy text) with placeholder \"GitHub username or profile URL\"\n- A \"Connect\" button next to it (Drafting Line Blue background, Chalk Paper text)\n- After connecting: show a small grid of repo cards (2-3 columns), each card showing: repo name in IBM Plex Mono, a short one-line description in Inter, and 2-3 tech stack tags styled as small pill badges in IBM Plex Mono on a Slate Fog background\n\n### 5. Job Description Section\n- Section label: \"Step 3 \342\200\224 The Job You Want\"\n- A styled text input for a job posting URL, placeholder: \"Paste a job posting link (LinkedIn, Naukri, Indeed)\"\n- A \"Fetch Job\" button (Drafting Line Blue background)\n- After fetching: show the job title (Space Grotesk, bold), company name (Inter, Slate Fog), and a short excerpt of the description in Inter\n\n### 6. Fit Analysis Results Section\n- Section label: \"Your Fit Analysis\"\n- A large overall verdict badge at the top: text like \"Strong Match\" (Verified Green background) or \"Partial Match\" (Signal Amber background) or \"Needs Work\" (a muted red, `#C4634F`, used only here)\n- Below it, a list of the student\'s existing projects as cards, each showing: project name (IBM Plex Mono), a fit verdict tag, and 1-2 lines of plain-language reasoning in Inter (e.g., \"Your e-commerce API project shows strong backend skills, but this role also wants ML experience which isn\'t reflected in your repos yet.\")\n- A small caption at the bottom of this section in Slate Fog, smaller text: \"This is AI-generated guidance to help you decide what to build next \342\200\224 not a guarantee of how a recruiter will see your profile.\"\n\n### 7. Roadmap Section\n- Section label: \"Your Build Plan\"\n- 2-3 recommended project cards, laid out as a horizontal drafted path (connected by a dashed line, similar styling to the hero diagram) on desktop, and a simple vertical stacked list on mobile\n- Each card shows: project title (Space Grotesk, medium), a one-sentence problem statement (Inter), tech stack tags (IBM Plex Mono pills), and an estimated build time (e.g., \"~1 week\", Slate Fog text)\n- Clicking a card expands it to show the full milestone-by-milestone roadmap (a simple numbered list is fine here \342\200\224 numbering is appropriate since these are genuinely sequential steps)\n\n### 8. Application Package Section\n- Section label: \"Ready to Apply\"\n- A card showing a tailored resume-highlight summary and a \"why this role\" blurb, each with a small \"Copy\" button (icon + text) next to it\n- A simple status selector below: three tappable pill buttons \342\200\224 \"Saved\", \"Applied\", \"Interviewing\" \342\200\224 the selected one filled with Signal Amber, others outlined\n\n## Interaction & Accessibility Requirements\n- All interactive elements (buttons, inputs, cards, the roadmap items) must have a visible focus state: a dashed Signal Amber outline, not the browser default\n- Fully responsive down to mobile width (375px): the hero diagram and roadmap path should both collapse to a vertical stacked layout on narrow screens\n- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`: disable the hero draw-in animation and any hover-transition effects for users with that OS setting enabled\n- Use real semantic HTML (proper `<button>`, `<input>`, heading levels) \342\200\224 not divs styled to look like interactive elements\n\n## Explicit Don\'ts\n- Do not add any fake status text, version numbers, \"system\" labels, or invented technical jargon anywhere on the page\n- Do not use a dark-mode-with-emerald/cyan-glassmorphism look \342\200\224 that is explicitly NOT the direction\n- Do not use only one font across the whole page \342\200\224 the three type roles must be visually distinguishable\n- Do not leave any default unstyled browser form elements (file inputs, buttons) \342\200\224 every interactive element must be custom-styled to match the palette above"
    }
  }
}
