steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1785332524
      nanos: 54380695
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "# Master Prompt: AI-Powered Job Search & Skill-Gap Assistant for Students\n\n## Project Overview\n\nBuild a full-stack web application that helps college and university students streamline their job search process. The app takes a student\'s resume and GitHub profile, analyzes them against target job descriptions, and provides three integrated capabilities:\n\n1. **Resume-based application assistance** \342\200\224 help students apply to companies with a tailored, ready-to-submit application package\n2. **GitHub project fit analysis** \342\200\224 evaluate whether a student\'s existing projects match a target job description\n3. **Project recommendation engine** \342\200\224 suggest new projects (with roadmaps and problem statements) to close identified skill gaps, enriched with real research about the target company\n\nThis is a standalone product, not an extension of any other tool. It should work end-to-end without depending on external teams or third-party project roadmaps.\n\n---\n\n## Tech Stack\n\n- **Backend:** Python, FastAPI\n- **Frontend:** React + TypeScript\n- **AI/LLM:** Gemini API (or configurable LLM provider) for analysis, generation, and reasoning tasks\n- **Storage:** SQLite for MVP (structured for easy migration to Postgres later)\n- **Auth:** Simple JWT-based auth for student accounts\n- **External integrations:** GitHub API (public repo/profile data), web search/fetch for company research\n\n---\n\n## Core Modules\n\n### 1. Resume Intake & Parsing\n- Accept resume upload (PDF/DOCX)\n- Parse into structured data: skills, education, experience, projects listed, contact info\n- Store parsed resume as structured JSON tied to the student\'s profile\n- This module should be self-contained \342\200\224 no dependency on any third-party resume-building tool\n\n### 2. GitHub Profile Analysis\n- Accept a student\'s GitHub username/link\n- Pull public repositories via GitHub API: README content, languages used, commit activity, stars/forks (as light signals only, not the main judgment criteria)\n- Summarize each repo into a structured \"project profile\": tech stack, apparent complexity, purpose/domain\n\n### 3. Job Description Intake (via existing scraper)\n- JD input is **not manual**. A job description scraper already exists and pulls postings from Naukri, LinkedIn, and Indeed \342\200\224 this module integrates with that scraper rather than asking the student to paste JD text.\n- Student provides a job posting URL (or selects from scraped/aggregated listings), and the scraper fetches the raw JD content\n- Extract structured requirements from the scraped JD: required skills, tech stack, seniority signals, domain (e.g., backend, ML, frontend), soft requirements\n- Handle scraper failures/edge cases gracefully (site structure changes, rate limits, listing taken down) \342\200\224 the pipeline should degrade cleanly (e.g., prompt for a different listing) rather than blocking the whole flow\n- Normalize JD data into one consistent structured format regardless of source site, since Naukri, LinkedIn, and Indeed postings differ significantly in structure and formatting\n\n### 4. Company Research Enrichment (optional layer, non-blocking)\n- Given a company name, attempt to gather public signal: engineering blog posts, tech stack mentions, recent product launches, official careers page details\n- This is an **enrichment layer only** \342\200\224 the system must produce a complete, high-quality result even when no company research is found (common case for smaller companies). Never let output quality depend on this step succeeding.\n- Cache company research results to avoid redundant lookups across students applying to the same company\n\n### 5. Fit Analysis Engine (core intelligence layer)\n- Compare parsed GitHub project profiles against the extracted JD requirements **and** available company research signal (engineering blog mentions, tech stack info, product domain) \342\200\224 not JD alone. A student\'s project can look like a strong JD match on paper but be a weak fit once real company context (e.g., \"this company is moving to microservices\" or \"this company is mobile-first\") is factored in, and vice versa.\n- When company research is unavailable (common case), fall back cleanly to JD-only comparison \342\200\224 this must never block or degrade the core output, per the enrichment-layer principle in Module 4\n- For each existing project, output: a fit score/verdict (strong match / partial match / weak match), and a plain-language explanation of why \342\200\224 explicitly noting whether the verdict was informed by company research or JD alone, so the student understands the basis of the judgment\n- Clearly flag this output as **advisory, not authoritative** \342\200\224 the UI copy should make clear this is a suggestion, not a definitive judgment, since GitHub signal (stale READMEs, incomplete context) and company research (often incomplete or absent) are both inherently imperfect\n\n### 6. Project Recommendation & Roadmap Generator\n- Based on gaps identified between the student\'s existing projects and the JD requirements (and company research, when available), generate:\n  - 2-3 recommended project ideas that would close the gap\n  - For each: a problem statement, a step-by-step roadmap (milestones, not just a task list), suggested tech stack, and an estimate of realistic build time\n- Roadmaps should be actionable enough that a student can start building the same day \342\200\224 not vague (\"learn Docker\") but concrete (\"build a service that does X, deploys via Docker, exposes endpoint Y\")\n\n### 7. Application Package Generator\n- Generate a tailored application package per job: a resume-highlight summary, a short \"why this role\" blurb, and any project-fit talking points, drawing on the fit analysis above\n- **Do not auto-submit to third-party portals.** Automated form-filling/scraping across arbitrary job sites is fragile and frequently violates platform terms of service. Instead:\n  - Where the app controls the surface (e.g., a direct partner-company integration or a direct email-application flow), allow one-click submission\n  - For everything else, produce a ready-to-copy/ready-to-submit package the student submits themselves\n- Track application status per student (applied, in progress, response received) as a lightweight tracker, not an automation engine\n\n---\n\n## Data Flow (high level)\n\n```\nResume Upload \342\206\222 Parse \342\206\222 Structured Resume\nGitHub Link \342\206\222 Fetch + Analyze \342\206\222 Structured Project Profiles\nJob Posting URL \342\206\222 Existing Scraper (Naukri/LinkedIn/Indeed) \342\206\222 Raw JD \342\206\222 Extract \342\206\222 Structured JD Requirements\nCompany Name (from JD) \342\206\222 Web Research (optional) \342\206\222 Company Signal Cache\n\nStructured Resume + Project Profiles + JD Requirements + Company Signal\n  \342\206\222 Fit Analysis Engine (JD + company signal, with clean fallback to JD-only)\n  \342\206\222 Project Recommendation & Roadmap Generator\n  \342\206\222 Application Package Generator\n```\n\n---\n\n## Non-Functional Requirements\n\n- **Reliability without enrichment:** every core output (fit analysis, roadmap generation) must work correctly with zero company research data available.\n- **Latency awareness:** the company research step involves multiple web calls; design it as an async/background enrichment that updates the result after initial generation, rather than blocking the main response.\n- **Clear UX disclaimers:** anywhere the app renders a fit judgment or suggestion, label it clearly as AI-generated guidance, not a guarantee.\n- **Modularity:** each module (resume parsing, GitHub analysis, JD extraction, company research, fit analysis, roadmap generation, application packaging) should be a separately callable service/function, independently testable, not one monolithic pipeline.\n\n---\n\n## MVP Scope for First Build\n\nBuild all modules above end-to-end for a single student flow:\n1. Student signs up, uploads resume, links GitHub\n2. Student provides a job posting URL (Naukri/LinkedIn/Indeed) \342\200\224 the existing scraper pulls the JD automatically; company name is extracted from the JD, not entered manually\n3. App returns: fit analysis of existing projects, 2-3 recommended projects with roadmaps, and a tailored application package\n4. Company research runs as enrichment and updates results if/when available\n\nKeep the UI simple: one flow, one results page. Polish and additional features (application tracking dashboard, saved job history, multiple resume versions) come after this core loop is validated.\nignore anything related to frontend since we have already created a frontend"
    items {
      text: "# Master Prompt: AI-Powered Job Search & Skill-Gap Assistant for Students\n\n## Project Overview\n\nBuild a full-stack web application that helps college and university students streamline their job search process. The app takes a student\'s resume and GitHub profile, analyzes them against target job descriptions, and provides three integrated capabilities:\n\n1. **Resume-based application assistance** \342\200\224 help students apply to companies with a tailored, ready-to-submit application package\n2. **GitHub project fit analysis** \342\200\224 evaluate whether a student\'s existing projects match a target job description\n3. **Project recommendation engine** \342\200\224 suggest new projects (with roadmaps and problem statements) to close identified skill gaps, enriched with real research about the target company\n\nThis is a standalone product, not an extension of any other tool. It should work end-to-end without depending on external teams or third-party project roadmaps.\n\n---\n\n## Tech Stack\n\n- **Backend:** Python, FastAPI\n- **Frontend:** React + TypeScript\n- **AI/LLM:** Gemini API (or configurable LLM provider) for analysis, generation, and reasoning tasks\n- **Storage:** SQLite for MVP (structured for easy migration to Postgres later)\n- **Auth:** Simple JWT-based auth for student accounts\n- **External integrations:** GitHub API (public repo/profile data), web search/fetch for company research\n\n---\n\n## Core Modules\n\n### 1. Resume Intake & Parsing\n- Accept resume upload (PDF/DOCX)\n- Parse into structured data: skills, education, experience, projects listed, contact info\n- Store parsed resume as structured JSON tied to the student\'s profile\n- This module should be self-contained \342\200\224 no dependency on any third-party resume-building tool\n\n### 2. GitHub Profile Analysis\n- Accept a student\'s GitHub username/link\n- Pull public repositories via GitHub API: README content, languages used, commit activity, stars/forks (as light signals only, not the main judgment criteria)\n- Summarize each repo into a structured \"project profile\": tech stack, apparent complexity, purpose/domain\n\n### 3. Job Description Intake (via existing scraper)\n- JD input is **not manual**. A job description scraper already exists and pulls postings from Naukri, LinkedIn, and Indeed \342\200\224 this module integrates with that scraper rather than asking the student to paste JD text.\n- Student provides a job posting URL (or selects from scraped/aggregated listings), and the scraper fetches the raw JD content\n- Extract structured requirements from the scraped JD: required skills, tech stack, seniority signals, domain (e.g., backend, ML, frontend), soft requirements\n- Handle scraper failures/edge cases gracefully (site structure changes, rate limits, listing taken down) \342\200\224 the pipeline should degrade cleanly (e.g., prompt for a different listing) rather than blocking the whole flow\n- Normalize JD data into one consistent structured format regardless of source site, since Naukri, LinkedIn, and Indeed postings differ significantly in structure and formatting\n\n### 4. Company Research Enrichment (optional layer, non-blocking)\n- Given a company name, attempt to gather public signal: engineering blog posts, tech stack mentions, recent product launches, official careers page details\n- This is an **enrichment layer only** \342\200\224 the system must produce a complete, high-quality result even when no company research is found (common case for smaller companies). Never let output quality depend on this step succeeding.\n- Cache company research results to avoid redundant lookups across students applying to the same company\n\n### 5. Fit Analysis Engine (core intelligence layer)\n- Compare parsed GitHub project profiles against the extracted JD requirements **and** available company research signal (engineering blog mentions, tech stack info, product domain) \342\200\224 not JD alone. A student\'s project can look like a strong JD match on paper but be a weak fit once real company context (e.g., \"this company is moving to microservices\" or \"this company is mobile-first\") is factored in, and vice versa.\n- When company research is unavailable (common case), fall back cleanly to JD-only comparison \342\200\224 this must never block or degrade the core output, per the enrichment-layer principle in Module 4\n- For each existing project, output: a fit score/verdict (strong match / partial match / weak match), and a plain-language explanation of why \342\200\224 explicitly noting whether the verdict was informed by company research or JD alone, so the student understands the basis of the judgment\n- Clearly flag this output as **advisory, not authoritative** \342\200\224 the UI copy should make clear this is a suggestion, not a definitive judgment, since GitHub signal (stale READMEs, incomplete context) and company research (often incomplete or absent) are both inherently imperfect\n\n### 6. Project Recommendation & Roadmap Generator\n- Based on gaps identified between the student\'s existing projects and the JD requirements (and company research, when available), generate:\n  - 2-3 recommended project ideas that would close the gap\n  - For each: a problem statement, a step-by-step roadmap (milestones, not just a task list), suggested tech stack, and an estimate of realistic build time\n- Roadmaps should be actionable enough that a student can start building the same day \342\200\224 not vague (\"learn Docker\") but concrete (\"build a service that does X, deploys via Docker, exposes endpoint Y\")\n\n### 7. Application Package Generator\n- Generate a tailored application package per job: a resume-highlight summary, a short \"why this role\" blurb, and any project-fit talking points, drawing on the fit analysis above\n- **Do not auto-submit to third-party portals.** Automated form-filling/scraping across arbitrary job sites is fragile and frequently violates platform terms of service. Instead:\n  - Where the app controls the surface (e.g., a direct partner-company integration or a direct email-application flow), allow one-click submission\n  - For everything else, produce a ready-to-copy/ready-to-submit package the student submits themselves\n- Track application status per student (applied, in progress, response received) as a lightweight tracker, not an automation engine\n\n---\n\n## Data Flow (high level)\n\n```\nResume Upload \342\206\222 Parse \342\206\222 Structured Resume\nGitHub Link \342\206\222 Fetch + Analyze \342\206\222 Structured Project Profiles\nJob Posting URL \342\206\222 Existing Scraper (Naukri/LinkedIn/Indeed) \342\206\222 Raw JD \342\206\222 Extract \342\206\222 Structured JD Requirements\nCompany Name (from JD) \342\206\222 Web Research (optional) \342\206\222 Company Signal Cache\n\nStructured Resume + Project Profiles + JD Requirements + Company Signal\n  \342\206\222 Fit Analysis Engine (JD + company signal, with clean fallback to JD-only)\n  \342\206\222 Project Recommendation & Roadmap Generator\n  \342\206\222 Application Package Generator\n```\n\n---\n\n## Non-Functional Requirements\n\n- **Reliability without enrichment:** every core output (fit analysis, roadmap generation) must work correctly with zero company research data available.\n- **Latency awareness:** the company research step involves multiple web calls; design it as an async/background enrichment that updates the result after initial generation, rather than blocking the main response.\n- **Clear UX disclaimers:** anywhere the app renders a fit judgment or suggestion, label it clearly as AI-generated guidance, not a guarantee.\n- **Modularity:** each module (resume parsing, GitHub analysis, JD extraction, company research, fit analysis, roadmap generation, application packaging) should be a separately callable service/function, independently testable, not one monolithic pipeline.\n\n---\n\n## MVP Scope for First Build\n\nBuild all modules above end-to-end for a single student flow:\n1. Student signs up, uploads resume, links GitHub\n2. Student provides a job posting URL (Naukri/LinkedIn/Indeed) \342\200\224 the existing scraper pulls the JD automatically; company name is extracted from the JD, not entered manually\n3. App returns: fit analysis of existing projects, 2-3 recommended projects with roadmaps, and a tailored application package\n4. Company research runs as enrichment and updates results if/when available\n\nKeep the UI simple: one flow, one results page. Polish and additional features (application tracking dashboard, saved job history, multiple resume versions) come after this core loop is validated.\nignore anything related to frontend since we have already created a frontend"
    }
