steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1785332714
      nanos: 170134182
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "# Supplementary Prompt: Auth Security, Data Privacy & Fit-Engine Testing\n\nAdd the following three pieces to the existing implementation plan for the Job Search & Skill-Gap Assistant. These are gaps in the current plan \342\200\224 implement them alongside the modules already scoped, not as a separate later phase.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Auth Security Specifics\n\nThe current plan mentions auth endpoints but doesn\'t pin down the hashing scheme or token lifecycle. Implement explicitly:\n\n- **Password hashing:** use `bcrypt` via `passlib` (or `argon2` via `argon2-cffi` if preferred \342\200\224 argon2 is the stronger modern default, bcrypt is more universally battle-tested; pick one and be consistent). Never store plaintext passwords, never use a fast general-purpose hash like SHA-256 for passwords.\n- **JWT handling:**\n  - Use short-lived access tokens (15\342\200\22330 min expiry) plus a longer-lived refresh token (7\342\200\22314 days), rather than one long-lived token \342\200\224 this limits damage if a token leaks.\n  - Store the JWT secret key in environment variables (already implied by `config.py`), never hardcoded.\n  - On login, issue both tokens. On access token expiry, the frontend should silently use the refresh token to get a new access token rather than forcing re-login.\n  - Implement a logout endpoint that invalidates the refresh token (store a revocation list or track valid refresh tokens in the DB \342\200\224 a simple `refresh_tokens` table with `user_id`, `token_hash`, `expires_at`, `revoked` is enough for MVP).\n- **Password requirements:** enforce a minimum length (8+ chars) server-side at registration \342\200\224 don\'t rely on frontend validation alone.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Data Retention & Privacy Note\n\nResumes and GitHub data are personal data (PII). Add this explicitly to the plan and implementation, even for MVP:\n\n- **Add a one-paragraph data handling note to the plan itself** (in the README or a `PRIVACY.md`): what\'s stored (resume content, GitHub username + public repo metadata, JD/company research), why it\'s stored (to generate fit analysis and roadmaps), and how long it\'s retained.\n- **Retention default:** for MVP, keep student data only as long as the account is active. Add a `deleted_at` soft-delete pattern on `User` and cascade-relevant data (StudentProfile, FitAnalysis, ApplicationPackage) so account deletion actually removes personal data rather than just marking the user inactive.\n- **Add an account deletion endpoint** (`DELETE /api/profile` or similar) that lets a student request full data deletion \342\200\224 this should be present even in MVP, not deferred, since resumes are sensitive data.\n- **Avoid storing raw resume files longer than needed for parsing** \342\200\224 store the parsed structured JSON as the source of truth, and either discard the raw uploaded file after parsing or store it only if there\'s a clear re-parsing need, with the same deletion rules applied.\n- **Company research data is not personal data** (it\'s about companies, not students) \342\200\224 no special handling needed there beyond normal caching.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Automated Unit Tests for the Fit Analysis Engine\n\nThe fit engine (`fit_engine.py`) is the highest-risk module for silent bugs because its output is AI-generated free text/structured reasoning \342\200\224 a bug here could produce a confidently wrong verdict that\'s hard to spot just by reading it. Add automated unit tests specifically for this module, beyond the manual end-to-end walkthrough already planned:\n\n- **Test the fallback behavior explicitly:** call the fit engine with company research data present, and again with it absent (`None`/empty), and assert:\n  - Both calls return a complete, valid response (no missing fields, no exceptions)\n  - The `informed_by_company_research` flag is set correctly in each case (`True` when research was used, `False` when it fell back to JD-only)\n- **Test with deliberately mismatched input:** a GitHub profile with clearly irrelevant projects (e.g., all frontend projects) against a JD requiring backend/ML skills \342\200\224 assert the verdict correctly comes back as a weak match, not a false positive. This catches cases where the LLM prompt is too lenient or the scoring logic defaults to \"match\" when uncertain.\n- **Test with a strong match case:** a profile with projects that clearly satisfy the JD \342\200\224 assert a strong-match verdict, to catch the opposite failure mode (overly harsh scoring).\n- **Test structural validity, not just content:** assert the response always matches the expected schema (`overall_score`, `match_verdict`, `project_evaluations_json`, `informed_by_company_research`) even when the underlying LLM call fails or times out \342\200\224 the engine should fail gracefully into a clear error state, never return a malformed or partial JSON blob to the frontend.\n- **Mock the Gemini API calls in these tests** (don\'t hit the live API in unit tests) so tests are fast, deterministic, and don\'t depend on API quota \342\200\224 use a fixture with canned LLM responses for the \"no research,\" \"with research,\" \"weak match,\" and \"strong match\" scenarios.\n\nThese tests should live alongside the existing test suite structure and run as part of the same automated test step already planned for backend verification \342\200\224 not as a separate manual step.\nignore anything related to frontend as we already have a frontend"
    items {
      text: "# Supplementary Prompt: Auth Security, Data Privacy & Fit-Engine Testing\n\nAdd the following three pieces to the existing implementation plan for the Job Search & Skill-Gap Assistant. These are gaps in the current plan \342\200\224 implement them alongside the modules already scoped, not as a separate later phase.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Auth Security Specifics\n\nThe current plan mentions auth endpoints but doesn\'t pin down the hashing scheme or token lifecycle. Implement explicitly:\n\n- **Password hashing:** use `bcrypt` via `passlib` (or `argon2` via `argon2-cffi` if preferred \342\200\224 argon2 is the stronger modern default, bcrypt is more universally battle-tested; pick one and be consistent). Never store plaintext passwords, never use a fast general-purpose hash like SHA-256 for passwords.\n- **JWT handling:**\n  - Use short-lived access tokens (15\342\200\22330 min expiry) plus a longer-lived refresh token (7\342\200\22314 days), rather than one long-lived token \342\200\224 this limits damage if a token leaks.\n  - Store the JWT secret key in environment variables (already implied by `config.py`), never hardcoded.\n  - On login, issue both tokens. On access token expiry, the frontend should silently use the refresh token to get a new access token rather than forcing re-login.\n  - Implement a logout endpoint that invalidates the refresh token (store a revocation list or track valid refresh tokens in the DB \342\200\224 a simple `refresh_tokens` table with `user_id`, `token_hash`, `expires_at`, `revoked` is enough for MVP).\n- **Password requirements:** enforce a minimum length (8+ chars) server-side at registration \342\200\224 don\'t rely on frontend validation alone.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Data Retention & Privacy Note\n\nResumes and GitHub data are personal data (PII). Add this explicitly to the plan and implementation, even for MVP:\n\n- **Add a one-paragraph data handling note to the plan itself** (in the README or a `PRIVACY.md`): what\'s stored (resume content, GitHub username + public repo metadata, JD/company research), why it\'s stored (to generate fit analysis and roadmaps), and how long it\'s retained.\n- **Retention default:** for MVP, keep student data only as long as the account is active. Add a `deleted_at` soft-delete pattern on `User` and cascade-relevant data (StudentProfile, FitAnalysis, ApplicationPackage) so account deletion actually removes personal data rather than just marking the user inactive.\n- **Add an account deletion endpoint** (`DELETE /api/profile` or similar) that lets a student request full data deletion \342\200\224 this should be present even in MVP, not deferred, since resumes are sensitive data.\n- **Avoid storing raw resume files longer than needed for parsing** \342\200\224 store the parsed structured JSON as the source of truth, and either discard the raw uploaded file after parsing or store it only if there\'s a clear re-parsing need, with the same deletion rules applied.\n- **Company research data is not personal data** (it\'s about companies, not students) \342\200\224 no special handling needed there beyond normal caching.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Automated Unit Tests for the Fit Analysis Engine\n\nThe fit engine (`fit_engine.py`) is the highest-risk module for silent bugs because its output is AI-generated free text/structured reasoning \342\200\224 a bug here could produce a confidently wrong verdict that\'s hard to spot just by reading it. Add automated unit tests specifically for this module, beyond the manual end-to-end walkthrough already planned:\n\n- **Test the fallback behavior explicitly:** call the fit engine with company research data present, and again with it absent (`None`/empty), and assert:\n  - Both calls return a complete, valid response (no missing fields, no exceptions)\n  - The `informed_by_company_research` flag is set correctly in each case (`True` when research was used, `False` when it fell back to JD-only)\n- **Test with deliberately mismatched input:** a GitHub profile with clearly irrelevant projects (e.g., all frontend projects) against a JD requiring backend/ML skills \342\200\224 assert the verdict correctly comes back as a weak match, not a false positive. This catches cases where the LLM prompt is too lenient or the scoring logic defaults to \"match\" when uncertain.\n- **Test with a strong match case:** a profile with projects that clearly satisfy the JD \342\200\224 assert a strong-match verdict, to catch the opposite failure mode (overly harsh scoring).\n- **Test structural validity, not just content:** assert the response always matches the expected schema (`overall_score`, `match_verdict`, `project_evaluations_json`, `informed_by_company_research`) even when the underlying LLM call fails or times out \342\200\224 the engine should fail gracefully into a clear error state, never return a malformed or partial JSON blob to the frontend.\n- **Mock the Gemini API calls in these tests** (don\'t hit the live API in unit tests) so tests are fast, deterministic, and don\'t depend on API quota \342\200\224 use a fixture with canned LLM responses for the \"no research,\" \"with research,\" \"weak match,\" and \"strong match\" scenarios.\n\nThese tests should live alongside the existing test suite structure and run as part of the same automated test step already planned for backend verification \342\200\224 not as a separate manual step.\nignore anything related to frontend as we already have a frontend"
    }
  }
}
