    user_response: "# Phase 2 Prompt: Harden Auth, Add Privacy Controls, Add Fit-Engine Tests\n\n**Context for Antigravity: the base app (backend + frontend, blueprint design, core flow) is already built and working.** This is a follow-up pass to add three specific things on top of the existing codebase. Do not rebuild or restructure anything that already works \342\200\224 only touch the files named below, and only for the changes described.\n\nDo these three in order, one at a time, verifying each works before moving to the next.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1: Harden Auth\n\n**Files to touch:** `backend/app/core/security.py` (create if it doesn\'t exist), `backend/app/api/auth.py`, `backend/app/db/models.py`\n\n1. Add password hashing using `passlib[bcrypt]` (or `argon2-cffi` \342\200\224 pick whichever is already a dependency if one is partially there, otherwise use bcrypt). If the existing `/api/auth/register` and `/api/auth/login` endpoints aren\'t already hashing passwords properly, fix that first before adding anything else.\n2. Add server-side validation on `/api/auth/register`: reject passwords under 8 characters with a clear error message.\n3. Update JWT issuing logic:\n   - Access token: 30 minute expiry\n   - Refresh token: 14 day expiry\n   - Issue both on login\n4. Add a `RefreshToken` table to `models.py` if not present: `id`, `user_id`, `token_hash`, `expires_at`, `revoked`.\n5. Add two new endpoints:\n   - `POST /api/auth/refresh` \342\200\224 accepts a valid refresh token, issues a new access token\n   - `POST /api/auth/logout` \342\200\224 marks the refresh token as revoked in the DB\n6. On the frontend, update `frontend/src/services/api.ts`: add a request interceptor that, on a 401 response, attempts one silent refresh via `/api/auth/refresh` and retries the original request once. If refresh also fails, redirect to login. Confirm `AuthContext.tsx` correctly stores/clears both tokens through this flow.\n\n**Verify:** register a new user with a short password (should fail with clear message), register with a valid password, log in, wait for access token to expire (or manually test with a shortened expiry), confirm the app silently refreshes instead of logging the user out, then log out and confirm the refresh token is rejected if reused.\n\n---\n\n## Step 2: Add Privacy Controls\n\n**Files to touch:** `backend/app/api/profile.py`, `backend/app/services/resume_parser.py`, `backend/PRIVACY.md` (new file)\n\n1. Create `backend/PRIVACY.md` with a short, plain-language note: what\'s stored (resume content, GitHub username + public repo metadata, JD and company research data), why (to generate fit analysis and roadmaps), and how long (kept while the account is active; deleted on account deletion).\n2. In `resume_parser.py`: after successfully extracting structured JSON from an uploaded resume, delete the raw uploaded file. If parsing fails partway through, do not delete the raw file until a successful parse is confirmed \342\200\224 don\'t lose the only copy of a resume to a bug.\n3. Add `deleted_at` (nullable timestamp) to `User` in `models.py`.\n4. Add `DELETE /api/profile` endpoint: sets `deleted_at` on the user, and cascades soft-deletion across `StudentProfile`, `FitAnalysis`, `ApplicationPackage`, and revokes all rows in `RefreshToken` for that user. Company research (`CompanyResearch` table) is not personal data and should NOT be deleted \342\200\224 it stays cached for other students.\n5. Make sure all existing queries that fetch a user or their data filter out soft-deleted users (`deleted_at IS NULL`), so a \"deleted\" account can\'t still log in or appear in results.\n\n**Verify:** create an account, upload a resume, confirm the raw file is gone from disk but the parsed data still works correctly in the fit analysis flow. Then call the delete endpoint and confirm: login is blocked, previously stored resume/GitHub/fit-analysis data returns empty/404 appropriately, and refresh tokens for that account no longer work.\n\n---\n\n## Step 3: Add Fit-Engine Unit Tests\n\n**Files to touch:** `backend/tests/test_fit_engine.py` (new file), possibly a small refactor of `fit_engine.py` if it\'s not currently structured to accept mocked LLM responses (e.g., if the Gemini client isn\'t injectable/mockable, add a thin wrapper so tests can substitute canned responses without hitting the live API).\n\nWrite these five tests using `pytest` with mocked Gemini responses (canned JSON fixtures, no live API calls):\n\n1. **JD-only fallback:** call the fit engine with company research absent \342\200\224 assert it returns a complete valid response and `informed_by_company_research == False`.\n2. **Enriched analysis:** call it with company research present \342\200\224 assert `informed_by_company_research == True` and that the research signal is reflected in the rationale.\n3. **Weak match:** feed in a GitHub profile with clearly irrelevant projects (e.g., only frontend projects) against a JD requiring backend/ML skills \342\200\224 assert the verdict comes back as a weak match, not a false positive.\n4. **Strong match:** feed in a profile with projects that clearly satisfy the JD \342\200\224 assert a strong-match verdict.\n5. **Schema/error fallback:** simulate the mocked LLM call failing or timing out \342\200\224 assert the engine returns a clear, structured error state rather than a partial or malformed response reaching the caller.\n\n**Verify:** run `pytest backend/tests/test_fit_engine.py` and confirm all five tests pass, and that they run without any network calls (mock everything).\n\n---\n\n## Order of operations reminder\n\nDo Step 1 fully, confirm it works, then Step 2, confirm it works, then Step 3. Don\'t start the next step until the current one is verified working \342\200\224 this is a hardening pass on a working app, not a rebuild, so keep changes scoped tightly to the files listed per step."
    items {
      text: "# Phase 2 Prompt: Harden Auth, Add Privacy Controls, Add Fit-Engine Tests\n\n**Context for Antigravity: the base app (backend + frontend, blueprint design, core flow) is already built and working.** This is a follow-up pass to add three specific things on top of the existing codebase. Do not rebuild or restructure anything that already works \342\200\224 only touch the files named below, and only for the changes described.\n\nDo these three in order, one at a time, verifying each works before moving to the next.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1: Harden Auth\n\n**Files to touch:** `backend/app/core/security.py` (create if it doesn\'t exist), `backend/app/api/auth.py`, `backend/app/db/models.py`\n\n1. Add password hashing using `passlib[bcrypt]` (or `argon2-cffi` \342\200\224 pick whichever is already a dependency if one is partially there, otherwise use bcrypt). If the existing `/api/auth/register` and `/api/auth/login` endpoints aren\'t already hashing passwords properly, fix that first before adding anything else.\n2. Add server-side validation on `/api/auth/register`: reject passwords under 8 characters with a clear error message.\n3. Update JWT issuing logic:\n   - Access token: 30 minute expiry\n   - Refresh token: 14 day expiry\n   - Issue both on login\n4. Add a `RefreshToken` table to `models.py` if not present: `id`, `user_id`, `token_hash`, `expires_at`, `revoked`.\n5. Add two new endpoints:\n   - `POST /api/auth/refresh` \342\200\224 accepts a valid refresh token, issues a new access token\n   - `POST /api/auth/logout` \342\200\224 marks the refresh token as revoked in the DB\n6. On the frontend, update `frontend/src/services/api.ts`: add a request interceptor that, on a 401 response, attempts one silent refresh via `/api/auth/refresh` and retries the original request once. If refresh also fails, redirect to login. Confirm `AuthContext.tsx` correctly stores/clears both tokens through this flow.\n\n**Verify:** register a new user with a short password (should fail with clear message), register with a valid password, log in, wait for access token to expire (or manually test with a shortened expiry), confirm the app silently refreshes instead of logging the user out, then log out and confirm the refresh token is rejected if reused.\n\n---\n\n## Step 2: Add Privacy Controls\n\n**Files to touch:** `backend/app/api/profile.py`, `backend/app/services/resume_parser.py`, `backend/PRIVACY.md` (new file)\n\n1. Create `backend/PRIVACY.md` with a short, plain-language note: what\'s stored (resume content, GitHub username + public repo metadata, JD and company research data), why (to generate fit analysis and roadmaps), and how long (kept while the account is active; deleted on account deletion).\n2. In `resume_parser.py`: after successfully extracting structured JSON from an uploaded resume, delete the raw uploaded file. If parsing fails partway through, do not delete the raw file until a successful parse is confirmed \342\200\224 don\'t lose the only copy of a resume to a bug.\n3. Add `deleted_at` (nullable timestamp) to `User` in `models.py`.\n4. Add `DELETE /api/profile` endpoint: sets `deleted_at` on the user, and cascades soft-deletion across `StudentProfile`, `FitAnalysis`, `ApplicationPackage`, and revokes all rows in `RefreshToken` for that user. Company research (`CompanyResearch` table) is not personal data and should NOT be deleted \342\200\224 it stays cached for other students.\n5. Make sure all existing queries that fetch a user or their data filter out soft-deleted users (`deleted_at IS NULL`), so a \"deleted\" account can\'t still log in or appear in results.\n\n**Verify:** create an account, upload a resume, confirm the raw file is gone from disk but the parsed data still works correctly in the fit analysis flow. Then call the delete endpoint and confirm: login is blocked, previously stored resume/GitHub/fit-analysis data returns empty/404 appropriately, and refresh tokens for that account no longer work.\n\n---\n\n## Step 3: Add Fit-Engine Unit Tests\n\n**Files to touch:** `backend/tests/test_fit_engine.py` (new file), possibly a small refactor of `fit_engine.py` if it\'s not currently structured to accept mocked LLM responses (e.g., if the Gemini client isn\'t injectable/mockable, add a thin wrapper so tests can substitute canned responses without hitting the live API).\n\nWrite these five tests using `pytest` with mocked Gemini responses (canned JSON fixtures, no live API calls):\n\n1. **JD-only fallback:** call the fit engine with company research absent \342\200\224 assert it returns a complete valid response and `informed_by_company_research == False`.\n2. **Enriched analysis:** call it with company research present \342\200\224 assert `informed_by_company_research == True` and that the research signal is reflected in the rationale.\n3. **Weak match:** feed in a GitHub profile with clearly irrelevant projects (e.g., only frontend projects) against a JD requiring backend/ML skills \342\200\224 assert the verdict comes back as a weak match, not a false positive.\n4. **Strong match:** feed in a profile with projects that clearly satisfy the JD \342\200\224 assert a strong-match verdict.\n5. **Schema/error fallback:** simulate the mocked LLM call failing or timing out \342\200\224 assert the engine returns a clear, structured error state rather than a partial or malformed response reaching the caller.\n\n**Verify:** run `pytest backend/tests/test_fit_engine.py` and confirm all five tests pass, and that they run without any network calls (mock everything).\n\n---\n\n## Order of operations reminder\n\nDo Step 1 fully, confirm it works, then Step 2, confirm it works, then Step 3. Don\'t start the next step until the current one is verified working \342\200\224 this is a hardening pass on a working app, not a rebuild, so keep changes scoped tightly to the files listed per step."
    }
  }
}
